WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:05.660
This episode is brought to you by the album
Music for Scientists, now available on all

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:21.539
streaming platforms.

00:00:21.539 --> 00:00:28.050
This strange-looking microbe is a Suctorian,
and it’s sort of the ugly duckling of ciliates.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:33.489
Not because they’re ugly, but because like
the poor fabled cygnet, suctorians do not

00:00:33.489 --> 00:00:44.100
bear a resemblance to the rest of their ciliate
family.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:49.179
For a eukaryote to be classified as a ciliate,
it really needs to meet one important criteria:

00:00:49.179 --> 00:00:52.980
it needs to have those short little hairs
called cilia.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:57.920
And with such lax requirements, it’s not
surprising that ciliates can take on so many

00:00:57.920 --> 00:00:59.469
different forms.

00:00:59.469 --> 00:01:03.079
You’ve got the long-necked lacrymaria olor.

00:01:03.079 --> 00:01:04.680
The trumpet-y stentor coeruleus.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:07.240
The springy florals of the vorticella.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:11.450
They may look very different from one another,
but they’re all decorated with cilia that

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:14.380
help them move or eat.

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:20.780
Meanwhile, there’s no sign of cilia on this
suctorian.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:23.829
And there’s none on this one either.

00:01:23.829 --> 00:01:29.280
They’re hairless, practically bald by microbe
standards…except for a few tentacles on

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:30.280
their head.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:35.520
And there’s some other weird traits that
distinguishes suctorians from other ciliates.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.119
So why consider them a ciliate?

00:01:39.119 --> 00:01:41.510
Well, we’ll get to that.

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:44.790
First, let’s talk about the tentacles.

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:47.530
This isn’t our first tentacle sighting in
the microcosmos.

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:54.119
We’ve seen them before on actinobolina,
made through a similar assembly of microtubules—though

00:01:54.119 --> 00:02:01.899
the actinobolina’s extensions look a lot
spikier next to the dotted end of the suctorian’s tentacles.

00:02:01.899 --> 00:02:07.220
Plus, unlike the spikes that radiate around
the entirety of the actinobolina’s body,

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:11.730
many suctorian species keep their tentacles
bundled together.

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:19.010
But physical differences aside, both organisms
rely on their tentacles for the same purpose: to hunt.

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:26.380
Both species use their tentacles to grab ahold
of their prey, and toxicysts to immobilize it.

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:31.280
For actinobolina, the next step is to drag
their food inwards, bringing it closer and

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:36.170
closer towards their version of a mouth, so
that they can begin eating.

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:42.760
But suctorians differ in one one crucial feature:
the mouth.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:44.220
They don’t have one.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:52.590
Or rather, they don’t have the oral cavity
that seems kind of mouth-like in other microbes.

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:58.490
But they do have a very special kind of tentacle
that lets them have something even better…many

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:04.900
mouths, which is a trait called polystomy.

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:07.280
Their tentacles are like a Swiss Army knife.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:10.810
When hunting, they act as a poison-tipped
harpoon.

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:16.330
And when eating, they act like a straw.

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:23.780
After the suctorian latches onto its prey,
the membrane of its tentacle merges with the

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:31.870
membrane of its food, creating a shared surface
that lets the suctorian begin to consume the

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:34.090
inside of its food.

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:40.470
That rounded end begins to form a pocket that
travels inwards, carrying bits of the suctorian’s

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:42.000
prey with it.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:48.670
So suctorians eat a bit weirdly for a ciliate,
and it turns out they also poop differently.

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:54.100
To get rid of waste, many ciliates have a
cytoproct, an opening that helps transport

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:56.860
material out of the organism when it’s done
eating.

00:03:56.860 --> 00:03:59.710
But suctorians do not have one.

00:03:59.710 --> 00:04:05.330
And no, we’re not about to follow this up
with a reveal like the many mouths they ended up having.

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:10.570
Suctorians do not have one butthole, nor do
they have many buttholes.

00:04:10.570 --> 00:04:16.260
Instead, different species had to devise their
own waste disposal methods.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:21.670
Some of them create little waste-specific
regions in their body, which then periodically

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:23.180
get removed.

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:26.430
Others get rid of waste at the base of their
tentacles.

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:33.260
But if they don’t get rid of that waste
fast enough, they can potentially feed themselves to death.

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:37.990
It’s just one of those reminders from the
microcosmos not to take the little things,

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:41.800
like being able to poop, for granted.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:47.790
And so between the hairlessness and the many
mouths and the lack of a cytoproct, why would

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:52.500
we even classify suctorians with ciliates
to begin with?

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:58.830
Alfred Kahl, the influential ciliate scholar,
didn’t even include them in his most important studies.

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:04.840
Well, let’s just watch this suctorian for
a bit—the oval with its tentacles latched

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:11.930
onto some unfortunate ciliate prey.

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:22.790
It’s eating and eating, but you can see
the suctorian begin to push some round thing

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:23.790
out of itself.

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:30.030
And then…a thing so exciting it caught our
master of microscopes James off guard for

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:36.830
a second there, but that little round thing
that came out is a swarmer, a little bitty

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:42.600
baby suctorian that has budded off its parent.

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:45.510
We sadly don’t have a lot of swarmers to
show you.

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:51.480
But that young suctorian is why they’re
considered ciliates because at this stage

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:56.490
in life, they have bands of cilia, which help
them move around.

00:05:56.490 --> 00:06:01.380
As they grow older, the suctorians settle
into a different form, like the ones we’ve

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:03.660
been watching for most of this video.

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:09.380
The maturing suctorians create stalks that
attach to whatever substrate seems most appealing,

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:12.740
which can even include the bodies of other
animals.

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:20.780
And that’s where they will stay, exchanging
cilia for tentacles, and swarming for sessility—a

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:22.630
lack of mobility.

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:27.730
But the young swarmer stage that came before,
however brief and strange it is compared to

00:06:27.730 --> 00:06:33.200
the rest of the suctorian’s life, is still
important, helping the species spread and

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:37.520
move beyond the stationary home their parent
created for themselves.

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:43.690
There are, after all, many ways to be anything,
including ciliates.

00:06:43.690 --> 00:06:50.050
And to be temporarily one is a means to the
suctorian’s own ends, a way of life that

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:56.790
has little concern for human understanding
or classification, but that delights us not

00:06:56.790 --> 00:07:10.490
in spite of its resistance to classification...but
because of it.

00:07:10.490 --> 00:07:16.990
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:23.169
Patrick Olson is a composer, musician, and
science publisher who just today released

00:07:23.169 --> 00:07:25.530
